# cdncheck — CDN/WAF/cloud classification for an IP (ProjectDiscovery).
# Built from a pinned source tag; VERSION is our published image tag, REF the
# exact upstream ref. Bump REF to upgrade; the pipeline re-runs govulncheck +
# Trivy before publishing.
REPO=https://github.com/projectdiscovery/cdncheck
VERSION=v1.3.1
REF=v1.3.1
PKG=./cmd/cdncheck
BIN=cdncheck
GO_VERSION=1.25.12
# No reachable govulncheck findings at v1.3.1 as of the initial build. Add
# `module@version` overrides here if the gate flags one upstream hasn't fixed.
GO_PATCHES=
#
# ─── Two things about this tool that are not obvious ────────────────────────
#
# 1. THE DATASET IS COMPILED IN, NOT FETCHED AT RUNTIME.
#    cdncheck `//go:embed`s `sources_data.json` (~2.4 MB of CIDR/ASN ranges)
#    into the binary at build time. "Auto-updating" therefore means "updates
#    when you rebuild", NOT "refreshes itself in production". That makes this
#    repo the update mechanism: the weekly schedule in build.yml re-runs the
#    security gate against this pin, and bumping REF is how the ranges move.
#    A long-lived deployment pinned to an old digest serves old ranges - so
#    treat a stale cdncheck pin the way you'd treat a stale dataset.
#
# 2. IT PHONES HOME ON EVERY RUN UNLESS YOU STOP IT.
#    Unless `-duc` / `-disable-update-check` is passed, cdncheck calls
#    ProjectDiscovery's version endpoint on every invocation
#    (internal/runner/options.go: `if !opts.DisableUpdateCheck`). Verified
#    with `--network none`: the check fails soft, so classification still
#    works without it - but every consumer MUST still pass `-duc`. An
#    outbound call per scan is latency we don't need, and a security product
#    should not phone home by default.
#
# ─── Why scratch is still the right base here ───────────────────────────────
#
# Upstream's own Dockerfile uses alpine + ca-certificates + bind-tools, so it
# is worth saying why we don't. Our use is IP -> {is_cdn, is_waf, is_cloud,
# provider} resolved purely from the embedded dataset: no DNS, no TLS, no
# network at all once `-duc` is set. Shipping without a CA bundle also means
# the phone-home fails closed even if a consumer forgets `-duc`, which is the
# safer default for this product.
#
# The trade-off, recorded so it isn't rediscovered the hard way: cdncheck's
# CNAME-based detection needs DNS, and its ipinfo ASN path needs TLS. Neither
# works in scratch. If a future consumer wants either, that needs a base with
# a CA bundle and a resolver - a deliberate change, not a silent one.
